>
  <h2>Context</h2>
  <p>
    mStable is a savings-and-yield product with real brand equity: years of history, a
    recognizable name, and users who remembered it — a brand that had gone quiet, but not one
    starting from zero. I owned <strong>both product and marketing</strong> for its relaunch
    as an automated yield product, one that deploys deposits across established markets and
    manages positions automatically.
  </p>

  <h2>Problem</h2>
  <p>
    Relaunching a dormant financial brand is a narrow path. Overclaim and you burn the trust
    that makes the brand worth relaunching; underclaim and nobody shows up. Worse, yield
    products face a cold-start problem — early depositors want evidence that others have
    already committed. Day one isn't just a milestone; it's the proof point that decides the
    trajectory.
  </p>

  <h2>Approach</h2>
  <h3>Position around the mechanism users actually value</h3>
  <p>
    We rebuilt the positioning around a single, concrete promise — <em>mStable efficiently
    stacks yield</em> — automated optimization across markets users already trusted, with no
    manual management. Every asset in the launch pointed at that one idea instead of a feature
    list.
  </p>
  <h3>Sequence the launch so day one is the crescendo</h3>
  <p>
    I planned the relaunch as a sequence rather than an announcement: line up the story,
    the product, and the partners so everything landed in the same window, concentrating
    attention where the cold-start problem is worst.
  </p>
  <h3>Launch with borrowed trust</h3>
  <p>
    I secured co-promotion from <strong>Ethena, Aave, and the Ethereum Foundation</strong> —
    three of the most credible names adjacent to the product. For a financial relaunch,
    endorsement-by-association from institutions users already trust does what no ad spend
    can: it collapses the "is this safe?" question on day one.
  </p>

  <h2>Outcome</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>$3.5M in deposits on the first day</strong> of relaunch.</li>
    <li>
      The day-one proof point solved the cold-start problem — later depositors arrived at a
      product that already looked chosen.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What this shows</h2>
  <p>
    Owning product and marketing together is a forcing function for honesty: the positioning
    has to be something the product actually does, and the product has to ship what the story
    promises. When one person owns both, launch day stops being a handoff and becomes a single
    designed event.
  </p>
</CaseStudyLayout>
